  {
    "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/whatwg/compat/issues/comments/1510519543",
    "html_url": "https://github.com/whatwg/compat/issues/240#issuecomment-1510519543",
    "issue_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/whatwg/compat/issues/240",
    "id": 1510519543,
    "node_id": "IC_kwDOAkXfh85aCLL3",
    "user": {
      "login": "karlcow",
      "id": 505230,
      "node_id": "MDQ6VXNlcjUwNTIzMA==",
      "avatar_url": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/505230?v=4",
      "gravatar_id": "",
      "url": "https://api.github.com/users/karlcow",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/karlcow",
      "followers_url": "https://api.github.com/users/karlcow/followers",
      "following_url": "https://api.github.com/users/karlcow/following{/other_user}",
      "gists_url": "https://api.github.com/users/karlcow/gists{/gist_id}",
      "starred_url": "https://api.github.com/users/karlcow/starred{/owner}{/repo}",
      "subscriptions_url": "https://api.github.com/users/karlcow/subscriptions",
      "organizations_url": "https://api.github.com/users/karlcow/orgs",
      "repos_url": "https://api.github.com/users/karlcow/repos",
      "events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/karlcow/events{/privacy}",
      "received_events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/karlcow/received_events",
      "type": "User",
      "user_view_type": "public",
      "site_admin": false
    },
    "created_at": "2023-04-16T23:38:04Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-04-16T23:42:16Z",
    "body": "Let's see online: [Github Search for `-webkit-text-security`](https://github.com/search?q=%22-webkit-text-security%22&type=code)\r\n\r\nit seems also [used for fingerprinting](https://github.com/Roll20/roll20-character-sheets/blob/ba70f6e672622420f899532716e512bfae6a550c/TheWitcher/witchersheet.css#L4710-L4717), I wonder how it might break firefox in another way, by selecting codepath which was not meant to be seen, but most of the time it seems to be used by itself.\r\n\r\nThere are some [interesting usage patterns in terms of UI](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/25822682/webkit-text-security-compatibility#answer-71149466) on why people are using it or more exactly why in some circumstances people do not want to use `type=\"password\"`\r\n\r\n1. want to have a hidden PIN code, but do not want the browser to prompt for saving it.\r\n2. The fact to be able to get a UI widget to enter numbers.\r\n3. Usage of specific fonts to hide the code. \r\n\r\nSome [hacks people rely](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/36935576/how-to-make-input-type-tel-work-as-type-password/36936452#36936452) on to try to get what they want, aka [revealing/hiding the input](https://css-tricks.com/the-options-for-password-revealing-inputs/).\r\n\r\nAlso the [intent to ship from Mozilla for [css-ui-4] Implement \"input-security\" CSS property](https://groups.google.com/a/mozilla.org/g/dev-platform/c/vikcWAyyAnc)\r\n\r\nBUT \r\n\r\n[CSS-UI-4](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-ui-4/#input-security) seems to want to remove the feature.\r\n\r\n>  The CSS-WG has agreed that while be believe that providing this piece of functionality to users is important, doing it via CSS+JS is the wrong approach, and that instead it should be built into user agents: this needs to work consistently from site to site for it to be discoverable and understandable by users, this needs to work even when JS is turned off, this needs to have consistenly solid accessibility… We therefore intend to remove this from the specification and instead, we would like to see this behavior specified in the HTML specification as part of the interaction model of password fields. Holding off deleting until the situation with HTML is clarified. See https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues/6788 and https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/7293.\r\n\r\n* [[css-ui] 'input-security' considered harmful](https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues/6788)\r\n* [<input type=password> should provide UI to show/hide its value](https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/7293)\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
